module newtype.pos_3

// AN ALIAS BEFORE ANOTHER TYPE, and this fixture exists because that shape was
// broken and the refusal LIED.
//
// Measured 2026-08-21 (wave B8 of sub-plan 9.1g.1, found while restructuring
// `collect_types` -- not by a probe, by reading): with `type Count alias int`
// ahead of `type Cel { v f64 }`, novac refused `c.v` with "unknown field: this
// type has no field with this name" -- about a field that is right there, on a
// file the oracle accepts. Delete the alias line and the same file compiled.
//
// THE CAUSE was a two-pass registry whose passes could disagree. Pass 1 handed
// out a declaration id per type NAME; pass 2 pushed the rows. An alias owns no
// row (it is the same type under a second name, so a row of its own would BE a
// distinct type) -- but it consumed an id, so every type declared after one had
// an id one greater than its row index, the `id != types.len()` guard skipped
// it, and the type silently vanished from the registry.
//
// THE FIX is not a better guard: pass 1 now BUILDS the row, so "the id is the
// row index" holds by construction and there is nothing left to guard. The alias
// is registered in pass 2, where its representation can be resolved -- it may
// point at a declaration further down the file.
//
// Kept as a fixture because the shape is ordinary: one `alias` anywhere above a
// record is enough, and a compiler that loses a type here is wrong about
// everything below it.
type Count alias int

type Cel { v f64 }

fn main() {
    ro c = Cel { v: 1.5 }
    println(c.v)
}
